  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-18",
    "page_path": "/explore/urban/",
    "name": "Transport, Cities & Public Infrastructure",
    "level": 1,
    "parent_path": "/explore/",
    "entity_type": "category",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's transport networks, housing, urban planning, and public utilities connect settlements and support everyday life. Understanding them requires attention to service access, maintenance, safety, environmental hazards, and the difference between infrastructure assets and functioning services.",
    "tags": ["Transport", "Cities", "Public infrastructure", "Housing", "Urban resilience", "Utilities"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:34:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:34:55Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Transport, Cities & Public Infrastructure in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran's transport networks, housing, planning, and public services, with attention to engineering heritage, access, safety, and resilience."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "connected-systems",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Infrastructure as a connected system",
        "content": "A country's infrastructure is easier to understand through the services it enables than through a catalogue of construction projects. A railway connects places, but its usefulness also depends on stations, connecting transport, maintenance, and the settlements it serves. UNESCO's account of the Trans-Iranian Railway illustrates this wider relationship: the line's significance encompasses engineering, trade, architecture, and connections between rural and urban life. [s1]\n\nThe same perspective applies within cities. UN-Habitat's Iran overview identifies challenges involving water and wastewater infrastructure, vulnerable urban lifelines and essential buildings, informal settlements, housing affordability, and deteriorating historic urban areas. Together, these concerns show why transport, housing, planning, and utilities belong in a common account of how settlements function. [s2]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "transport-and-terrain",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Transport networks and engineering heritage",
        "content": "The Trans-Iranian Railway offers a concrete introduction to the relationship between geography and engineering. Constructed between 1927 and 1938, the historic route connects the Caspian Sea with the Persian Gulf across mountain ranges and contrasting landscapes. UNESCO records a length of 1,394 kilometres and emphasizes the bridges, tunnels, and route design required to negotiate difficult terrain. This is the extent of the heritage railway, not the length of Iran's entire present-day rail network. [s1]\n\nRoad transport belongs to a different but connected network. ESCAP, the United Nations regional commission for Asia and the Pacific, treats the Asian Highway, Trans-Asian Railway, and internationally significant dry ports as components of an integrated transport system. This framework helps explain the relationship between roads, railways, and freight transfer points without implying that a designated corridor guarantees uninterrupted service or uniform infrastructure quality. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "planning-and-housing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Housing and planning shape access",
        "content": "UN-Habitat's diagnosis places housing affordability and physical and socioeconomic vulnerability alongside infrastructure deficiencies. It also identifies inefficient rural–urban links and the deterioration of historic urban fabric. These issues draw attention to the distribution of infrastructure benefits: a city can possess substantial physical assets while some residents still face difficult access to suitable housing and essential services. [s2]\n\nThe directory therefore separates urban planning from housing while keeping their connections visible. Planning concerns the spatial organization of settlements and activities; housing concerns the dwellings and conditions through which people inhabit them. The branch pages should examine those subjects with local evidence rather than assuming that a national description captures every city or neighbourhood. UN-Habitat's overview provides a starting diagnosis, not a current comparative assessment of all Iranian settlements. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "risk-and-continuity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Resilience means keeping services working",
        "content": "CADRI, an interagency partnership supporting disaster-risk capacity, describes an Iranian risk landscape that includes earthquakes and landslides as well as floods, drought, forest fires, and sand and dust storms. It also identifies technological hazards associated with industrial development, including chemical spills and dam failures. Its country account records a support mission in 2020 and work on practical guidance, including resilient infrastructure and urban water supply. [s4]\n\nThese hazards make service continuity a useful lens for reading infrastructure policy. The question is not simply whether an asset exists, but whether the service it supports can withstand disruption and recover. CADRI's account supports attention to multiple hazards rather than an earthquake-only view of resilience; it does not establish the present condition of individual networks. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "contemporary-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A dated infrastructure baseline matters",
        "content": "In March 2026, the United Nations Development Programme reported that military escalation was disrupting livelihoods and essential services, including through damage to water, electricity, health, and education infrastructure. Its analysis combined available information with preliminary scenario simulations. The distinction matters: reported disruption is not the same kind of evidence as a modelled future impact, and neither supplies a complete asset-by-asset assessment at the October research cutoff. [s5]\n\nThis contemporary context limits what older infrastructure descriptions can establish. A historical network map or engineering account remains useful for understanding development, but it cannot verify current service availability. A fuller overview requires dated operational evidence for road, rail, air, and maritime transport as well as housing, urban services, and utilities. [s1][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "directory-guide",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to explore this subject",
        "content": "Use the transport branches to distinguish movement between places from the organization of life within them. Read urban planning and housing together, then examine urban public services and infrastructure for the systems supporting daily routines. Across these branches, compare access, reliability, safety, and resilience as well as physical scale. That reading approach follows the combined emphasis of the inspected heritage, urban-development, and disaster-risk sources, while leaving current performance claims to evidence specific to each system. [s1][s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
