  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-18-1",
    "page_path": "/explore/urban/road-transport/",
    "name": "Road Transport",
    "level": 2,
    "parent_path": "/explore/urban/",
    "entity_type": "category",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Road transport in Iran encompasses highways, freight terminals, buses, intercity passenger services, and traffic-management systems. Network extent alone cannot establish performance: safety, vehicle condition, border procedures, and the quality of operational data also matter.",
    "tags": ["Road transport", "Road safety", "Highways", "Freight", "Intercity transport", "Transport data"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:34:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:34:55Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Road Transport in Iran: Networks, Services & Safety | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iranian road transport through network functions, regional connections, road-safety evidence, and the limits of available transport statistics."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "network-and-service",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read the road system at several scales",
        "content": "Road transport includes the physical network and the services operating on it. The distinction matters when reading this directory: freeway and highway pages concern infrastructure, freight terminals concern transfer and organization, and bus fleets and intercity transport concern vehicles and passenger services. Traffic-management systems add another layer of operation. These subjects should be assessed separately before drawing conclusions about the road system as a whole.\n\nAt the regional scale, ESCAP describes the Asian Highway as a cooperation framework for improving road infrastructure and operations. Its wider transport approach links roads with railways and dry ports, which are inland freight facilities connecting transport networks. An international route designation therefore identifies a network relationship; it is not a statement that all sections offer identical design standards, capacity, or journey conditions. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-operations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Infrastructure and transport operations are different questions",
        "content": "A United Nations report on the third Ministerial Conference on Transport records Iran's identification of missing road and rail links, ageing vehicles and rolling stock, complicated border procedures, and insufficient investment as obstacles to efficient transport. This is historical evidence of the issues raised by Iran at that conference, not a verified inventory of present-day deficiencies. [s2]\n\nThe distinction remains useful for interpreting development claims. Building or upgrading a road addresses a physical constraint; it does not by itself establish fleet quality or resolve border administration. Conversely, an operational improvement may benefit movement without adding road kilometres. The conference record shows why transport assessment needs to examine infrastructure, equipment, and procedures together. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "safety-burden",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Safety is a central measure of performance",
        "content": "WHO's Iran profile accompanying the Global status report on road safety 2023 distinguishes nationally reported deaths from WHO mortality estimates. For 2021, the profile records 16,778 reported road traffic deaths and estimates 18,156 deaths, with an uncertainty interval. These figures describe the same reference year through different statistical approaches; they are not successive points in a trend. [s3]\n\nThe profile also reports the existence of road-safety standards and assessment arrangements. Such indicators describe institutional provisions and should not be read as proof that every road is safe or every requirement is consistently implemented. The dataset below retains the distinction between the reported death count and WHO's estimate. It does not infer causation from the existence of a law or standard. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "safety-reference-table",
        "type": "table",
        "content": {
          "title": "Reported and WHO-estimated road traffic deaths, 2021",
          "dataset_id": "road-traffic-deaths-2021"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "data-and-users",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Who is affected, and what the data can support",
        "content": "The Asian Transport Observatory's September 2025 profile emphasizes the continuing burden of road crashes and differences between mortality sources. It also identifies limitations in available infrastructure safety ratings and vehicle-fleet information. These gaps restrict how confidently a reader can connect national outcomes to particular road designs or changes in motorization. [s4]\n\nIts discussion of pedestrians and cyclists also broadens the subject beyond vehicle occupants. A road network serves people with different levels of physical protection, so an account focused only on freight speed or car travel would miss an important part of transport performance. The profile supports that wider perspective, but its graphs have not been converted here into invented annual observations. Exact underlying values are needed before publishing a reproducible trend chart. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-branch",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a fuller account needs",
        "content": "The branch pages should connect infrastructure classifications to dated evidence on actual services. Freeway and highway coverage need clear definitions; bus and freight accounts need vehicle and activity measures; traffic-management claims need evidence about operation and outcomes. ESCAP's network framework, the historical conference record, and the inspected safety profiles establish useful questions, but do not supply a complete current statistical account. [s1][s2][s3][s4]\n\nFor that reason, this page remains a research draft. Its strongest quantitative evidence is a clearly labelled mortality snapshot. Current traffic volumes, fleet condition, passenger activity, and freight performance require further verification before the page can support a comprehensive assessment through October 2026.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [
      {
        "id": "road-traffic-deaths-2021",
        "title": "Reported and WHO-estimated road traffic deaths in Iran, 2021",
        "geography": "Iran (Islamic Republic of)",
        "population_scope": "People dying from road traffic injuries within the national scope used by the WHO country profile",
        "frequency": "annual",
        "calendar": "gregorian",
        "methodology": "Single-year snapshot from the BURDEN section of the WHO Iran country profile accompanying the Global status report on road safety 2023. Nationally reported fatalities and WHO-estimated fatalities are retained as separate series. WHO conducted mortality estimation; other country-profile information was collected through its survey and cleared by government-designated focal points. The two series must not be treated as successive years or added together. Exact reporting-period boundaries are not supplied in this dataset.",
